<!--
    The widget palette, kept beside the launcher rather than sent from Dart.

    The payload carries tone NAMES — "fine", "soon", "late_" — and these decide
    what they look like. A colour crossing the channel as a hex string would be
    a second copy of the palette, and the one nobody remembers to change.

    ── Copied from lib/ui/theme.dart, value for value ─────────────────────────

    These were eyeballed at first and drifted: the gold here was #B07D18 where
    the app's is #9A6B0F, and the surface was flat white where the app's cards
    are slate800. Close enough to look right alone and wrong beside the app,
    which is the worst of both.

    So each line below is a literal from theme.dart, named for the field it
    came from. This file is the LIGHT one — the launcher picks values-night
    when the phone is dark — so these are the second value of each pair there.

        widget_surface   slate800    a widget is a card on a wallpaper
        widget_text      text
        widget_muted     muted
        widget_line      line
        widget_gold      gold        the "it" in the wordmark
        widget_moss      moss        tone: fine
        widget_honey     honey       tone: soon
        widget_ember     ember       tone: late

    There is no way to make the compiler check that. What there is: one comment
    saying where they came from, so the next person to change theme.dart knows
    there is a second copy and where it lives.
-->
<resources>
    <color name="widget_surface">#FFFFFFFF</color>
    <color name="widget_text">#FF22252A</color>
    <color name="widget_muted">#FF6B7079</color>
    <color name="widget_line">#FFD5D1C6</color>

    <color name="widget_gold">#FF9A6B0F</color>
    <color name="widget_moss">#FF2F7D4F</color>
    <color name="widget_honey">#FF8A6A08</color>
    <color name="widget_ember">#FFB83C28</color>
</resources>
